Make the strength a product of honest factors, each from 0 to 1, rather than a switch.

(1) SUN UP AND DIRECT. 0 below -0.83 deg. Under a veiled or overcast sky use the scene's own litK and veil:
- above ~70-85% cloud, or a veiled sun: 0;
- thin cloud (~40-70% or a high veil): about half strength, narrower and paler, soft shadow, no shafts;
- rain, thunder or dense fog: 0.

(2) THE SUN IS BEHIND OR NEAR THE PART IN THE FRAME. The glow is forward scattering, so it falls with angular distance from the sun. On the phone frame:
- full within about a quarter crown-width of the disc;
- half by about half a crown-width;
- gone by about one crown-width.
Rim and translucency read this per scallop, and the moss per curtain.

(3) SUN HEIGHT. This sets colour and length more than presence: 0-6 deg is golden hour, rose then amber, with the longest shadows. 6-20 deg is pale butter-gold, the 8:35 case. As the disc climbs clear of the crown, the effect retreats to the crown's top edge, and past ~25-30 deg it hands back to ordinary side and top light. The cast shadow is about height/tan(altitude) long, toward the viewer.

(4) MOISTURE ON THE MOSS. Dry gives silver halos. Rain in the last few hours, or heavy fog or drizzle, makes the moss greener with a weaker, greener-gold halo.

LIGHT SHAFTS need all of these at once:
- the sun up and direct;
- the sun low, under about 20-25 deg;
- gaps between the eye and the sun;
- scatterers in the air;
- a dark backdrop behind the beam (the crown's shade, the shaded bank).
They show only over darks, never against the sky. Strength by air:
- clear dry air (RH under ~70%, no haze): none;
- humid haze (RH ~85-95%, mainly clear, calm, like this morning at 88%): a whisper or none;
- river mist, or fog thinning with the sun out (fog code 45/48, or the scene's fog with a sun that still shows): strong, the classic beams through the moss;
- dense fog with no sun, overcast, rain, or the sun past ~25 deg: none.

THIS MORNING'S CASE (2026-10-16T08:35, sun ~12.5 deg ESE, code 1, cloud 12, RH 88, wind 3 gusting 6, dry):
- rim and moss glow full;
- translucency medium;
- the back crown lighter than the front;
- birds rimmed only near the sun;
- a long shadow off the frame toward the viewer;
- wet-mud sheen outside the shadow;
- shafts at a whisper at most;
- warm haze on the far line toward the sun.

AT NIGHT: a bright moon behind the oak is far too faint for any of this. At most a thin cool silver line on the moss nearest it, and no translucency, no shafts, no sunstar. Leaving it off is honest.
